In Vercel￼ you normally don’t run Bash scripts manually like on a VPS server with SSH access.

Instead, there are 3 common ways:

⸻

1. Run scripts during deployment (most common)

You put scripts inside:

package.json

Example:

{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "node build.js",
    "test": "node test.js",
    "cleanup": "node cleanup.js"
  }
}

Then Vercel automatically runs:

npm run build

during deployment.

You can also configure:

* prebuild
* postbuild
* install hooks

⸻

2. Run scripts locally with Vercel CLI

Install CLI:

npm install -g vercel

Then:

vercel dev

or

npm run cleanup

This runs locally on your machine, not inside Vercel cloud.

⸻

3. Run server-side scripts as Vercel Functions

This is probably what you mean with function.js.

Example structure:

api/
  cleanup.js

Example:

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  console.log("Running cleanup");
  res.status(200).json({ success: true });
}

Then deployed URL becomes:

https://yourdomain.vercel.app/api/cleanup

Opening that URL RUNS the function.

⸻

There is NO persistent Bash path like:

/usr/bin/bash

because:

* Vercel is serverless
* functions are ephemeral
* you don’t SSH into the machine

So you can’t really:

ssh server
bash script.sh

like traditional hosting.

⸻

If you need Bash specifically

You can execute shell commands inside Node.js:

import { exec } from "child_process";
exec("ls -la", (error, stdout, stderr) => {
  console.log(stdout);
});

But Vercel limits:

* execution time
* filesystem access
* background processes

So this is usually only for lightweight automation.

⸻

For your RSVP project

You probably want one of these:

Cleanup cron job

Delete old RSVPs automatically.

Email retry job

Retry failed emails.

Expired invite cleanup

Archive old parties.

⸻

BEST WAY on Vercel: Cron Jobs

Vercel supports scheduled functions now.

Example:

{
  "crons": [
    {
      "path": "/api/cleanup",
      "schedule": "0 3 * * *"
    }
  ]
}

This runs every day at 3 AM.

Then:

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  // delete old rows from Supabase
  res.status(200).send("Cleanup complete");
}

That’s the modern serverless equivalent of a Bash cron script.

